The behavior described ("it pulls items up initially, but un-sticks after I click down once") confirms a Data Type Error.
The initial `customMarginTop` of the neighbor is `null` or `undefined`.
The calculation `null + GRID_UNIT` results in `NaN`.
The update fails silently, causing the "Reverse Snowplow" effect.

Please REPLACE the Nudge Logic in `handleMoveUp` with this EXACT code block that enforces `Number()` casting and nullish coalescing:

// --- REPLACE NUDGE LOGIC IN handleMoveUp ---

// 1. Safe Cast Current Margin
const currentMargin = Number(currentProduct.customMarginTop ?? 0);

if (currentMargin > STANDARD_GAP) {
    // 2. Calculate New Margin for Current Item (Moving Up)
    const newCurrentMargin = Math.max(0, currentMargin - GRID_UNIT);

    // 3. Identify and Update Neighbor (Counter-Balance)
    // We must find the visual neighbor strictly below logic
    const nextItem = sortedProducts.find(p => p.id === currentProduct.ceilingId) 
                     || sortedProducts[currentIndex + 1]; // Fallback to array index

    if (nextItem) {
        // CRITICAL FIX: Force cast to Number. Treat null/undefined as 0.
        const nextMargin = Number(nextItem.customMarginTop ?? 0);

        // Calculate counter-balance (Increase neighbor margin so it stays frozen)
        const newNextMargin = nextMargin + GRID_UNIT;

        // Execute Update
        onUpdateProduct(nextItem.id, 'customMarginTop', newNextMargin);
    }

    // Update Current Item
    onUpdateProduct(currentProduct.id, 'customMarginTop', newCurrentMargin);
    return; // Stop execution (Do not Swap)
}
// -------------------------------------------

Why this fixes it:
Using `Number(val ?? 0)` guarantees we never get `NaN`, even if the database returns raw nulls.

